00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.349 align:start position:0%
in singapore preparations are underway  as the asia pacific's key security  summit returns to the city-state after a  two-year hiatus due to covet-19  restrictions  it will provide a face-to-face encounter  between senior u.s and chinese defense  officials amid growing tensions  the summit is named for the hotel  hosting the event and is organized by  the london-based international institute  for strategic studies or the double iws  the main topic of the shangri-la  dialogue is the u.s and china relations  between the two superpowers are bad and  getting worse and so the two defense  ministers from the country will both be  speaking  now in its 19th year the meetings have  become a key forum for debating the  region's security challenges  the conference has traditionally  attracted attention not just for the  speeches and more public aspects but for  the bilateral and multilateral meetings  that take place quietly on the sidelines  until now the u.s and chinese defense  chiefs have only spoken on the phone  one of the big uh  talking points of this week is whether  lloyd austin the u.s defense secretary  and general wayfong her the chinese  defense secretary are actually going to  meet each other for the very first time  and so those meetings are important  they're the way that business gets done  in international defense diplomacy  washington has repeatedly stressed the  importance of the indo-pacific and  accused beijing of aggressive actions in  the region particularly over the south  china sea and taiwan  beijing has also criticized the u.s for  playing what it calls geopolitical games  with the region over issues such as a  security pact between australia the uk  and the us known as orcas which will  help australia to acquire  nuclear-powered submarines i think for  the first time indonesia analysts say  middle powers in the region are hoping  tensions can be resolved  we need to ask whether or not  competition could be de-escalated is it  managed or is it going to be one and i  think these are the questions that are  still not being fully answered but i  think from the perspective of southeast  asian states at least in an ideal world  we would like to see the us china  competition not only peacefully managed  but hopefully de-escalated and resolved  regional issues such as the violence in  myanmar with hundreds of civilians  killed since the 2021 coup will also be  discussed but with no representatives of  the junta or the lawmakers in exile  invited  organizers hope for a productive weekend  that leads to solutions rather than  further tensions jessica washington al  jazeera singapore
 

